    ],
    [
     3,
     "<div dir=\"auto\"><div dir=\"auto\"><div class=\"gmail_extra\" dir=\"auto\"><div class=\"gmail_quote\"><blockquote style=\"margin:0 0 0 .8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div>On 06/25/2017 09:32 AM, Mahima Raina wrote:<br>\r\n<blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0 0 0 .8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\">\r\nAlso , please see this<br>\r\n<br>\r\nAttaching package: ‘sem’ The following objects are masked from ‘package:lavaan’: cfa, sem<br>\r\n<br>\r\n<br>\r\nIs this relevant ?<br>\r\n</blockquote>\r\n<br>\r\n<br></div>\r\nYes, very much so. It means that you have loaded another package (sem), which overwrites the lavaan functions cfa/sem.<br>\r\n<br>\r\nEither do not load the sem package (at least not in the same R session as you are using lavaan), or use the lavaan:: prefix to call lavaan&#39;s cfa() function, as in<br>\r\n<br>\r\nfit &lt;- lavaan::cfa(model1,data=mydata1)<font color=\"#888888\"><br>\r\n<br>\r\nYves.</font></blockquote></div></div></div></div>"
    ],
